course: everyday
lesson: 510
title: Cousins: like a sister, posh, or far away
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your cousins are the children of your aunts and uncles. In Glasgow, Kenzie and her cousin Lyra live next door to each other and go everywhere together, so at school people think they're sisters. «Naw, cousins,» says Kenzie, «but same thing.» In Leicester, Arnav's cousin Rohan, 15, is like a big brother: he teaches him cricket, takes him to the shops and helps with his homework. Cousins who grow up close can be as close as siblings, or a role model you look up to.
explain: Some cousins live a different life. In Texas, Josiah's family lives in a trailer (a mobile home). At Thanksgiving his cousins from California talk about private schools and skiing, and one says, «You're so lucky, you don't have to do anything.» Josiah feels small and angry. Rich kids don't always understand, and Josiah can say calmly what his life is really like. In Cheshire, Lily-Grace's cousins talk dead posh and ride horses; she feels awkward, has a go on a pony and falls straight off into the mud. They all laugh, Lily-Grace loudest. Posh or not, cousins are kids too.
explain: Some cousins live far away. Every Sunday Mei-Lin in London video calls her cousin in Shanghai; the cousin teaches her Chinese words, and Mei-Lin teaches her slang: «That's peng» (London for really nice). Oskar moved from Kraków to Dundee, and messages his cousins on WhatsApp in Polish and English, missing the summers they played together. In Dublin, Rian's cousins visit from Australia: «Let's go to the servo for a snag!» Rian: «The what for a what?» (a servo is a petrol station, a snag is a sausage). Just ask what words mean.
explain: In Auckland, Mihi's whānau (Māori for extended family) is huge: at a family gathering there are 60 cousins and she doesn't know half of them. «Kia ora, cuz!» (kia ora is a Māori hello). In many cultures big, close families are normal, and the trick is to say hi and ask names. In Birmingham, Precious's cousins go to each other's parties but never invite her. Mum just says, «Family politics.» Sometimes a grown-up row keeps cousins apart; Precious can ask Mum gently, and it is not her fault.
explain: Not every cousin is a good idea. In Liverpool, Dafydd's cousin, 13, swears, smokes and tells him to nick sweets from the shop («nick» means steal). Dafydd's mum says, «You're not going round there.» Dafydd is annoyed, then quietly relieved. Saying no to things that feel wrong is right, even when it's family.
fact: Cousins are the children of your aunts and uncles.
fact: Cousins who grow up near each other can be as close as brothers and sisters.
fact: An older cousin can be a role model, like a big brother or sister.
fact: Cousins can grow up with very different amounts of money; rich children don't always understand, and having less doesn't make you less.
fact: Cousins abroad can stay close through calls and messages, and each can teach the other words.
fact: Shanghai is 8 hours ahead of London in winter, so a London morning is a Shanghai evening.
fact: Cousins in different countries use different slang; asking what a word means is normal.
fact: In many cultures, including Māori, extended families are huge and close; «whānau» means extended family and «kia ora» means hello.
fact: Grown-up rows sometimes keep cousins apart; it is not the child's fault.
fact: Some cousins are a bad influence; saying no to something wrong is right, even with family.
fact: «Servo» means petrol station and «snag» means sausage in Australia; «naw» is Scots for no; «nick» means steal; «dead posh» means very posh.
exercise: Why do people at school think Kenzie and Lyra are sisters? | They go everywhere together | They have exactly the same birthday | They both have the same surname | Same school bag
exercise: What did Kenzie say about Lyra? | «Naw, cousins, but same thing» | «She's my sister, we just don't say» | «I barely even know her, to be honest» | «Just my neighbour»
exercise: What does Arnav's cousin Rohan do with him? | Teaches cricket, helps with homework | Only tells him what to do all the time | Ignores him at family parties | Borrows his pocket money and keeps it
exercise: Josiah's cousin says, «You're so lucky, you don't have to do anything». What's going on? | A rich cousin who doesn't understand | A cousin who knows exactly how Josiah lives | A cousin being kind and helpful | A cousin who wants to swap houses
exercise: What can Josiah do? | Say calmly what his life is like | Pretend he lives in a mansion too | Never go to Thanksgiving ever again | Tell his cousin skiing is stupid
exercise: What happened when Lily-Grace tried a pony? | She fell off and laughed with everyone | She won a big rosette at the show | Her cousins wouldn't let her near it | She refused to get on in the first place
exercise: What does Mei-Lin teach her cousin in Shanghai? | London slang | Chinese words | Maths homework | Horse riding
exercise: What is a «snag» in Australia? | A sausage | A small shop | A fast car | A guard dog
exercise: How does Oskar keep in touch with his cousins in Poland? | WhatsApp in Polish and English | Letters in the post every year | He doesn't, they've lost touch | Only at Christmas by phone
exercise: What does «whānau» mean? | Extended family | A Māori hello | A distant cousin | A way to say bye
exercise: About how many cousins does Mihi not know at the gathering? | About 30 | About 60 | About 6 | About 15
exercise: Why might Precious not be invited to her cousins' parties? | A row between the grown-ups | Precious was rude to them last year | Her cousins don't like her clothes | She lives too far away to come
exercise: What did Dafydd's cousin tell him to do? | Nick sweets from the shop | Help his mum with the shopping | Play football | Stay out until midnight with him
exercise: How did Dafydd feel when his mum said «You're not going round there»? | Annoyed, then relieved | Only happy, right away | Angry for a whole year | Scared of his mum
test: Mei-Lin calls her cousin at 11 in the morning on a Sunday, London time, in winter. What time is it in Shanghai? | 7 in the evening, Sunday | 3 in the morning, Sunday | 7 in the morning, Monday | 11 in the morning, Sunday
test: Mei-Lin wants to call at 6 in the evening on Sunday, London time, in winter. What's the trouble? | It's 2 in the morning on Monday in Shanghai | It's lunchtime in Shanghai, she'll be eating | It's still Sunday afternoon in Shanghai | There's no trouble at all, it's the same time
test: Mihi has 60 cousins and knows half. She meets 18 new ones at the gathering. How many does she still not know? | 12 | 18 | 30 | 42
test: Dafydd's cousin: «go on, nick us some sweets, it's only a Mars, don't be a baby». What's right? | Say no, even to family | Do it, he's family | Do it just this once so you fit in | Nick a small one so nobody notices
test: Rian: «what's a servo, is it like a robot?» What's the truth? | A petrol station | A robot waiter | A kind of sausage they have on toast | A school for kids in the outback
test: «Your cousins are posh and you're not, so they're better than you». Right? | No, money doesn't make anyone better | Yes, posher people are just better | Yes, unless you are a lot cleverer | No, poorer people are always better
test: «Precious's cousins never invite her, she must've done summat bad». What's often really going on? | A grown-up row, not her fault | She must have been mean to them | They forgot her | She was never really their cousin
test: What would most likely happen if Oskar stopped messaging his cousins in Poland for years? | They might drift apart | They'd become closer than ever | Nothing would ever change | They'd forget Polish
test: In a film, the rich cousin is evil and the poor cousin is a saint. What's truer? | Rich or poor, cousins are just kids | Rich kids are always mean and selfish | Poor kids are always perfect and kind | Cousins with different money can't be friends
test: Arnav: «Rohan's like my big bro, innit». Can a cousin be like a big brother? | Yes, cousins can be role models | No, only real brothers count as that | No, cousins are always each other's rivals | Only if they live in the same house
test: Mihi meets a cousin she's never seen: «Kia ora, cuz!» What's she saying? | Hello, cousin | Goodbye, cousin | Who are you? | Go away
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between cousins raised in close proximity? | It may be as close as that of siblings | It is invariably distant and formal in character | It is discouraged in the majority of cultures | It is invariably characterised by rivalry
exam: Identify the most appropriate response of a child to a cousin who incites him to commit theft. | To refuse, notwithstanding the family relationship | To comply in order to preserve family harmony | To comply on a single occasion only | To commit the theft and subsequently confess
exam: Ascertain the most probable explanation, as stated in the lesson, where a child is persistently excluded from the gatherings of her cousins. | A dispute between adults | A grave fault of the child | Accidental omission on each occasion | The absence of any family relationship
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning cousins of markedly differing financial circumstances. | Wealth confers no superior worth, though it may limit understanding | The wealthier cousin is necessarily the superior person | The poorer cousin is necessarily the superior person | Cousins of differing means cannot be friends
